===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|informal}} An [[emotionally]] [[charged]] [[film]], [[novel]], [[song]], [[opera]], television [[episode]], etc., usually with one or more [[sad]] passages or ending, so termed because it suggests one is likely to [[cry]] during its performance.
#: {{synonyms|en|cryfest|sobfest}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2003|author=Steven Pinker|authorlink=Steven Pinker|title=How the Mind Works|publisher=Penguin |isbn=9780141927879|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=xhIgYdSq64gC&pg=PT448&dq=%22tearjerkers%22&hl=&cd=25&source=gbs_api#v=onepage&q=%22tearjerkers%22&f=false|text=The psychologist Paul Rozin lumps '''tearjerkers''' with other examples of benign masochism like smoking, riding on roller coasters, eating hot chili peppers, and sitting in saunas.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author={{w|Kitty Empire}} [pseudonym]|title={{w|The Streets}} review&nbsp;– the agony and ecstasy of a great everyman|editor={{w|Katharine Viner}}|newspaper={{w|The Guardian}}|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20190408062100/https://www.theguardian.com/music/2019/jan/26/the-streets-mike-skinner-review-o2-academy-leeds|archivedate=8 April 2019|location=London|publisher=[[w:Guardian Media Group|Guardian News & Media]]|date=26 January 2019|issn=0261-3077|oclc=229952407|passage=The many '''tear-jerkers''' deal with finality, with death and the end of love, with a stoicism pregnant with feeling.}}

